module bindings.python.cffi_callback_native_slice_write;

// cffi's `ffi.callback(c_signature, func)` hands a Python function to C, and
// the parameters that signature types as pointers arrive as native memory
// owned by the C caller: their capacity is fixed on the C side and carries no
// Python-side spelling at all, so Python writes through them without any
// bounds check of its own. A slice assignment whose extent is `len()` of the
// value being written is bounded by that value rather than by the
// destination, so a callback returning more bytes than the C buffer holds
// writes past it -- while the same slice assignment into a Python-level
// buffer raises instead, which is why the destination's nativeness is the
// whole judgement and the registration is the anchor.
//
// The registration and the write are never in the same function: the write
// sits in the nested callback body and `ffi.callback` is called by the
// enclosing one. A nested function's context does not reach the enclosing
// scope's calls, but a function's context inherits its nested functions'
// tokens, so the pair is observable only from the function that performs the
// registration, and that is where the finding is reported. Within that one
// function the two clauses are co-membership rather than a relation, so a
// registration and an unrelated length-sliced copy in the same function also
// report; the cffi anchor is what keeps that set small, and the copy idiom
// the anchor does not guard -- a chunked read into a Python buffer -- has no
// registration in it.
//
// The bound is a comparison in the function, in either operand order, which
// is the idiom used to guard a fixed C buffer; the negation is on the
// operators rather than on any bound's name, so a guard written against any
// spelling of the capacity discharges it. A return annotation (`-> int`) is
// not a comparison and does not.
//
// Residuals, all false-negative: the extent must be spelled `len(...)` inside
// the subscript, so a bound computed a line earlier (`n = len(v);
// out[0:n] = v`) leaves no token, and a clamp inside the subscript
// (`out[0 : min(len(v), cap)]`) changes the spelling; any comparison anywhere
// in the enclosing function, including one unrelated to the write,
// discharges it; and the `@ffi.def_extern()` spelling of the registration is
// invisible because the frontend extracts no call node for a decorator.
binding cffiCallbackNativeSliceWrite {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "python" and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["ffi.callback"]) and containsAny(node.context.subscript, [":len("]) and not containsAny(node.context.expr, [">", "<"])
  emit issue code.RawMemoryCopySize at node
}
